%        11 categorical + 7 numeric attrs, 10 outlier targets. Two scenarios: national
%        (s_B=s_C=0.8, o_BC=0.8) and study (s_B=s_C=0.2, o_BC=0.2). Grid: k-Anonymity k in
%        {2,5}, Average Risk {5%, 20%, 50%}, t-Closeness t=0.5, 2-Diversity crossed with
%        global/local generalization, microaggregation, cell suppression. Key findings:
%        (1) detectable signals only from outlier targets (Fig. 3, p. 5); (2) transformation
%        method matters as much as privacy model - microaggregation leaves strongest signals
%        regardless of k-Anonymity parameter (Figs. 4-5, pp. 6-7; sec. 5.2-5.3); (3) 2-Anonymity
%        + local generalization gives reasonable trade-off (sec. 5.4, p. 8; sec. 6.1, p. 9);
%        (4) 0.5-Closeness protects attribute inference but not membership inference under a
%        dominant sensitive category (sec. 5.3, p. 7). Explicit scope: tool for investigating
